  "assembled_on": "2026-11-11",
  "sealed": true,
  "window": {
    "start": "2026-10-01",
    "end": "2026-10-31"
  },
  "signal_types": [
    "funding",
    "leadership"
  ],
  "public_url": "https://github.com/dk-forge/talent-intelligence-tracker/blob/main/analysis/recall/us/goldset-us-2026-10.json",
  "what_this_measures": "United States employers that announced a funding round or a leadership change between 2026-10-01 and 2026-10-31, on the announcement date, broken out by metro (San Francisco Bay Area, New York City, Austin, rest of the US).",
  "method": "Assembled on 2026-11-11 by one assembling agent that ran the research passes one after another, each pass enumerating its own publishers in announcement-date order inside the window and taking events in that order. No pass consulted asktherecruiter.com, this repository, the tracker database or its source registry. Discovery was public web search and the publishers' own listing pages and sitemaps, read only where the site's robots.txt allows an automated reader (sites that refuse one, including some national dailies and the main US startup wires, were not used). Every row cites the original publisher: the company's own release, an exchange or company announcement, or a named outlet's own article. Every source page was fetched and the company, date, place and amount were read on the page before the row was written, and every URL was fetched again mechanically before sealing (all answered HTTP 200). Nothing was added, edited or removed after matching began, because matching has not begun. Eight passes were run: one per metro for funding and one per metro for leadership. Rest-of-US was capped at three events per other metro. Amounts not stated in US dollars are converted at fixed approximate rates (EUR 1.10, GBP 1.30, NOK 0.095, INR 1 crore = USD 119,000, AUD 0.66, NZD 0.58) and the detail field keeps the amount as published, so an amount check should allow for currency rounding.",
  "held_out": "Sampled independently of our own coverage: no pass saw our database, stored rows or collector list.",
  "sampling_by_cell": {
    "austin_funding": 7,
    "austin_leadership": 2,
    "nyc_funding": 13,
    "nyc_leadership": 1,
    "rest_funding": 15,
    "sf_funding": 14
  },
  "caveats": [
    "Leadership is thin: the leadership passes found verifiable October announcements for Austin and New York (three rows in all) and none that could be placed in San Francisco from an allowed publisher, so the US figure is mostly a funding figure.",
    "Several of the main US startup wires and city business journals refuse automated readers in robots.txt and were not used; trade coverage leans on the publishers that allow it, so press releases are close to half the set.",
    "Austin rows come largely from one local outlet's daily roundups, so the Austin cell is narrower in source than the others.",
    "A dateline gives the place of the release; where a release was datelined in a metro it is placed there.",
    "Fifty-two events give a worst-case 95% interval about 26 points wide; a metro cell is an indication, not a rate."
  ],
